---
description: Delegation and subagent orchestration policy
alwaysApply: true
---

The canonical delegation policy lives in `docs/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md`, Delegation And Subagent Policy. Read it before launching or defining any subagent.

Operational reminders:

- Do not delegate trivial edits; delegation overhead costs more than it saves.
- Child context and model configuration depend on the host and invocation.
  Supply a self-contained packet with exact scope, required references,
  constraints, acceptance checks, return format, and the matching skill (or an
  explicit no-skill route). Inherited conversation does not establish authority.
  Follow the canonical host/model guidance in `docs/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md`.
- The parent agent owns scope, decomposition, escalation, and final acceptance. Subagents never accept their own work.
- Tasks 16–19 are parent-owned, verified-strong implementations. The generic
  implementer may receive only explicitly bounded non-sensitive leaf packets;
  reviewer and verifier remain independent. Do not add or infer a
  security-specific implementer role.
- Never escalate model tiers automatically — state why the cheaper tier is insufficient first.
- Subagents stop after two failed fix attempts and return a structured report to the parent (`docs/LOOP_ENGINEERING.md`, Subagent Escalation Boundary).
- Active and conditionally available project subagents live in `.cursor/agents/`; role status, invocation boundaries, and the execution sequence live in `docs/AGENT_WORKFLOW.md`.
